  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The balanced chemical equation for the reaction between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HBr</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ba</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>HBr</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Ba</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>BaBr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p><p>From this equation, we can see that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HBr</mi></math> react with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> mole of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ba</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></math>.</p><p>Equal equivalents will react.&nbsp;</p><p>Number of equivalents = Normality&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#215;</mo></math>Volume</p><p>So,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>ml</mi></math></p></blockquote>"
